.he "disk1 (4)                   10/15/26                    disk1 (4)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
disk1
- a floppy disk on a CompuPro DISK 1 or DISK 1A controller
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
The
.bd
disk1
device reads and writes a floppy diskette attached to a CompuPro
DISK 1 or DISK 1A controller.  The board is an Intel 8272 (NEC uPD765)
floppy disk controller - the chip that owns the head, seeks it, finds
a sector's address mark and moves its data - behind a little CompuPro
glue: a 24 bit dma address register, a drive status register, and a
motor register that also turns the board's boot EPROM off.

The four ports are one block, set by DIP switch S3 and fixed at 0xc0
in every package CompuPro shipped:

.in +5
.nf
0xc0  read   the 8272's main status register
0xc0  write  the drive select register
0xc1  read   the 8272's data register
0xc1  write  the 8272's data register
0xc2  read   the drive status register
0xc2  write  the dma address register, three bytes, most significant
              byte first
0xc3  write  the motor control register
.in -5
.fi

Every byte of a command and every byte of an answer passes through the
one data register at 0xc1, and the main status register at 0xc0 is how
the cpu asks whether the chip is ready for it.  The DISK 1A carries
both drive banks at this one block, four eight inch drives and four
5 1/4 inch ones, and the bank is chosen by the drive select register's
data rate bit, which also sets the chip's rate to match the drive.

The device number packs two things.  The major number is 9, and the
low three bits of the minor number name the drive: 0 to 3 are the
eight inch bank, 4 to 7 the 5 1/4 inch, as the DJ-DMA numbers its
drives.

.in +5
.nf
9/0  d1a        9/4  d1e
9/1  d1b        9/5  d1f
9/2  d1c        9/6  d1g
9/3  d1d        9/7  d1h
.in -5
.fi

Those eight appear again 64 higher, as their whole-disk slices: 9/64
is d1ac, the drive at cylinder 0 and unrotated, so that its block 0 is
physical block 0 and reading it reads the diskette's label.

A file system block is one 512 byte sector, written in mfm, and one
request moves one of them: the driver puts the unit, the head, the
cylinder and the sector into a nine byte command, hands the board the
buffer's physical address through the dma register, and waits for the
chip to interrupt.  Unlike the DJ-DMA, whose board runs a program out
of memory, this transfer is interrupt driven, so the time a seek takes
is time the machine spends on something else.

The 8272 has one interrupt, and what it means depends on what was
issued.  After a recalibrate or a seek it means the head has arrived,
and the driver answers it with SENSE INTERRUPT STATUS, which is the
only thing that both reads that answer and clears the interrupt.  After
a read or a write it means the seven result bytes are waiting to be
read out of the data register.  Only one of the two is ever
outstanding, so one variable in the driver tells the handler which it
is.

The geometry comes off the diskette, from the label
.bd
mkfs
writes into the second half of the boot sector, so the driver has no
table of formats to disagree with the disk in the drive.  A diskette
that carries no Micronix label still opens on its whole-disk slice, so
that its raw sectors can be read and written.

A raw command block can be issued to this controller through
.bd
ioctl (2),
which is what a formatter needs: the driver looks at nothing in the
block but the length of the answer, so a FORMAT TRACK command with its
own header table is passed through as it stands.  The one thing the
caller cannot supply is the buffer's physical address, and the driver
fills that in.  A raw command runs to completion before it returns,
rather than interrupting.

The card has no reset line from the host, so a board that stops
answering is handled by failing the request that is stuck, three
seconds having passed, and forgetting which bank the chip's rate was
set for and which drive was selected; the next request sets both
again.  A read or a write that comes back an error is re-issued, up to
ten times, and halfway through the ten the driver homes the drive and
starts the request over, because the head and not the medium is the
usual reason a command fails.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
